    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born outside marriage near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, Juana Inés taught herself from her grandfather's books, was tested before the viceroy's learned guests as a teenager, and in 1669 entered the Hieronymite convent so that she could study as she wished.</li><li>In the convent she wrote the philosophical poem First Dream, love poems, comedies and villancicos, and corresponded with and was supported by viceroys and vicereines who had her work published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</li><li>After the Carta Atenagórica was published without her permission in 1690 and she answered her critics in the Respuesta, she was reprimanded, and by 1693 she seemingly stopped writing; she died in 1695 after nursing her sisters in a plague.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Sor Juana is said to have learned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> in only twenty lessons, and she reportedly cut her own hair as a punishment whenever she failed to learn something she had set herself.</p></aside>
    </section>

